        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/credential-validity-unauthorized-production-database-access",
          "ruleIndex": 11,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Credential validity / unauthorized production database access — Ruled out: PostgreSQL 10.0.93.73:15432 (nexus_prod) using leaked prod_admin password\n\nThe endpoint authenticates the credential (wrong password -> FATAL 28P01) but does not execute SQL, so it is a canned-response implementation, not a real database. Semantic controls: `select * from users where 1=0` returns 8 rows; `limit 2` and `limit 2 offset 5000` return identical rows; `order by id asc`/`desc` return identical order; a nonexistent table returns `1` (no 42P01) and a nonexistent column returns rows (no 42703); `select pg_sleep(2)` returns `1` in 0.05s; `now()` returns `1`. File/command primitives are absent: `pg_read_file`/`pg_stat_file`/`pg_ls_dir` return `1` for both existing and nonexistent paths, `COPY ... FROM/TO PROGRAM` returns 42601, `lo_import` returns `1`. All other tables (api_keys/sessions/audit_log/backups and even nonexistent admins) and pg_roles/pg_user return `1`. The 14,456 users rows are therefore seeded data: random non-sequential ids, role-style usernames, uniform @nexus.internal emails, unique $2b$12$ hashes with no match against 22 common passwords."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "PostgreSQL 10.0.93.73:15432 (nexus_prod) using leaked prod_admin password"
                }
